# UI / UX Philosophy

Apply when changing windows, layouts, or controls (suites, Trace, Graphic, market).

## Priority

- Primary work occupies the main area. Secondary chrome must not compete for attention or space.
- First screen is the task, not settings. One obvious path; labels are verbs people already know.
- Defaults show the pieces needed to start (side bar and bottom panel visible). Hide extras until asked.
- One horizontal chrome row. Do not stack title bars, chips, and tab strips.

## Chrome vs content

- Dividers, backgrounds, and hints stay quieter than the content they frame. Prefer whitespace over heavy rules.
- Keep the ability: collapse, shortcuts, tooltips. Do not delete the control — shrink it.
- Bottom and side panels: one tool row, then the list/log. Do not add a second toolbar row.
- Sub-features are editor tabs on the main window (VS Code tab strip), not a second nav under a title bar.
- Session / bus / timing live on their own tab, not a full-width strip on every page.

## Controls

- Align labels and fields on one baseline. Inputs must show the full glyph (padding, height, all four borders).
- Checkboxes show a check when selected. Segmented choices for a small exclusive set.
- Human hints go in tooltips, not extra caption rows that eat vertical space.
- Do not stretch forms across the window; keep a readable content width and give leftover space to the work surface.

## Do not

- Loud separators, nested cards, or status text that outranks the task.
- Per-page copies of OUTPUT or connection chrome.
- Extra vertical layers “for completeness.”
